# Line endings.
#
# Git stores every text file in this repo with Unix (LF) endings. Editors and
# tools on Windows tend to write files back with Windows (CRLF) endings, which
# made every file show up as modified in `git status` even when nothing in it
# had actually changed. That hides real work and turns `git add .` into a way
# to commit hundreds of lines of nothing.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells git to convert CRLF to LF when staging and to check
# files out as LF. Working files on Windows may still end up CRLF on disk, and
# that is fine: git now ignores the difference instead of reporting it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files must never be passed through line-ending conversion, which would
# corrupt them. `text=auto` detects most of these on its own; listing them is
# belt and braces.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary

# SVG is text, so it benefits from the same normalisation.
*.svg text eol=lf

# desktop.ini is a Windows system file and Windows expects it as it is.
desktop.ini -text
